WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.892
The classroom architecture is small, but it is not a toy.

00:00:03.892 --> 00:00:09.527
A production system begins with connectors and parsers instead of our simple CSV.

00:00:09.527 --> 00:00:14.242
It may use OCR, web crawlers, or database APIs.

00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:17.612
Chunking may become a document-processing service.

00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:20.972
Embeddings may come from a hosted model endpoint.

00:00:20.972 --> 00:00:25.747
The vector store may be a managed search platform with access-control filters.

00:00:25.747 --> 00:00:31.857
Retrieval may combine dense vectors, keyword search, and reranking.

00:00:31.857 --> 00:00:35.627
The prompt may be assembled by an orchestration service.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:40.977
The final answer includes citations and may be logged for evaluation.

00:00:40.977 --> 00:00:47.782
Then companies add monitoring, authentication, scheduled re-indexing, cost controls, and security reviews.

00:00:47.782 --> 00:00:51.607
The core idea, however, is still the same one you built in Part 1.

00:00:51.607 --> 00:00:57.337
Documents become searchable representations, relevant evidence is retrieved for a question,

00:00:57.337 --> 00:01:03.642
and the language model generates an answer from that evidence.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:07.628
Production adds scale and controls around the same information flow.
